Actually I think of the monk as the primary fighter-spellcaster.

Some possible things about the gentleman adventurer:

* Good boxer, fencer, swordsman (expert saber, dagger, bare hand
(master?), expert or skilled longsword, scimitar; so on)
* Good rider (skilled?)
* Eschews heavy armor - to-hit penalties
* Advanced education - unrestricted in all spell classes, expert in
divination, healing, expert or skilled in escape; basic or skilled in
others - warned before reading difficult spellbook
* Slowly acquires Intrinsics that reflect the "action hero" who can
get out of trouble - poison resistance, sickness resistance,
searching, stealth, food appraisal, perhaps warning, perhaps magical
breathing, perhaps speed, perhaps protection from shape changers
* Moderate HP
* Can unlock w/o a tool perhaps?
* Can identify curse? perhaps starting at mid levels
* Warned before eating self to death
* Starts with a new nerdy tool/gadget - the chemistry set? An
encyclopedia? (whatever that might do)
* Starts with a "tweed jacket"?
* Perhaps an "expedition vest" that is a piece of outerwear that is
also an ordinary bag

I dunno, all just ideas.

Pixie dust would primarily serve the purpose of increasing enchantment
that couldn't be increased via enchant armor/weapon/charging.

Again, I just want to add things that create new entertaining
alternatives in play, not ones that have a significant impact on
overall play. I don't want the game to take longer (if you don't want
to mess with additional levels) or become more frustrating, don't want
to close up "holes" that allow time-honored strategies like pudding
farming, so on.

